			<div class="content" id="post-content-4764881">
									

<p><strong>What I enjoyed:</strong> I like the premise. I like the connection to <a href="/scp-3456">SCP-3456</a> and the greater Orcadia canon. I'm intrigued by the background details given and discussed in this discussion thread. I find the merwoman, and portions of the article which relate to her, super interesting.</p>
<p><strong>What I didn't enjoy:</strong> I don't like how the parts of this article come together; I think the writing leaves much to be desired. I didn't understand the distinctions among the types of animals; why they were of significance; why lines on the ground could make animals <em>not</em> drown;<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-657363-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-657363-1')">1</a></sup> or, why the article delved so deep into their properties but never explained their connection to the merwoman.</p>
<p>I also didn't think the merwoman's intent came through in the article as well as it could have. I'm inferring she used the bullet-feathers to battle the Nuckelavee in those three sites of historical tragedy and returned gravely wounded. I couldn't infer why she decided to kill herself and the other train passengers, especially when it seems she was already dying from her injuries? There really wasn't a connection there, and I wish there were, because again &#8212; the premise has me really intrigued.</p>
<p>What I would <em>love</em> is an SCP about the merwoman, her effects of personal/cultural significance, and what she does. But I don't think it works too well here because it seems presented as an after-thought. (Then again, I'm still reading through the Orcadia canon, so maybe things will more sense later).</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
